      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear. The following qualification regulates its measure and basis but does not remove its truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The occurrence substantially invokes and affirms Cordelia’s existing filial bond rather than creating a new promise. “According to my bond” positions her love as governed by an obligation, and her continuation identifies the corresponding duties as obedience, love, and honour. This contextual material explicates the force of this occurrence itself, rather than supplying an unrelated nearby undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, measured answer in which Cordelia explicitly limits and explains what she says. The evidence does not present it as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled boundary-setting response to Lear’s competitive demand: Cordelia refuses to inflate or claim exclusive love, limiting her answer to what her filial relation warrants.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while distributing portions of his kingdom. After refusing ornamental amplification, Cordelia answers him directly with a measured avowal tied to her filial bond and then explains the duties and limits of that bond.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial obligation to love, obey, and honour Lear; she does not make a new promise or undertake unlimited devotion. Lear does not accept this bounded formulation as satisfactory: he tells her to “Mend your speech a little,” so the affirmation receives adverse uptake in the immediate exchange.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports represented affection through Cordelia’s avowal and asides, but it does not independently establish every practical term of her filial bond. It also does not show any later fulfilment or breach, which is unnecessary for classifying the original utterance.\"}"
        }
      ],
